From d040b61051aff11271c8f3912dd46de66b37ed92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: purplewall1206 <purplewall1206@github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 04:05:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] mm: CortenMM arena: prctl registration, per-mm frame xarray,
 shadow-VMA

M3b.S1-S3 of the CortenMM port (publish/M3B_DESIGN.md sec 2/3/6): the
opt-in arena layer gets its user-facing registration, the per-mm state
that backs it, and the shadow-VMA representation that keeps the rest of
the VMA-consuming kernel working over arena memory (decision D1) -- no
fault hooks yet, those land in S4-S6.

- prctl(PR_CORTEN_ARENA = 79): DECLARE/RELEASE/QUERY over
  [addr, addr + len) (uapi/linux/prctl.h, kernel/sys.c), CAP_SYS_ADMIN
  only.  DECLARE validates page alignment and private-anonymous VMA
  coverage and stages the shadow-VMA in place; RELEASE matches an
  exactly-covering arena and drains it; QUERY reports the state of a
  range.  Overlap checks precede shape checks (a sub-range DECLARE of
  an existing arena reports -EEXIST), all under ctl_lock (design
  sec 2.2/2.3).
- per-mm lifecycle: mm->corten_state (mm_types.h) carries the xarray
  of 2M-frame entries and a percpu_ref; the pointer is published with
  smp_store_release()/smp_load_acquire() so the fault path (S4+) can
  look it up without the mmap lock; release is a two-phase
  unpublish-then-drain (percpu_ref_kill waits out in-flight
  tryget_live users before teardown frees the state), and exit_mmap
  folds to corten_arena_mm_exit via the vm initializer chain
  (kernel/fork.c, design sec 2.4/2.5, sec 6 lock order).
- shadow-VMA: a declared range's VMA gains VM_CORTEN (bit 43 on
  CONFIG_64BIT; VM_NONE elsewhere) | VM_NOHUGEPAGE plus an
  "corten-arena" anon_vma_name, set in place (VM_NOHUGEPAGE is the
  single switch that keeps khugepaged/THP collapse off arena memory,
  design sec 3.2).  The generic flag comparison in is_mergeable_vma()
  doubles as the merge exclusion: a shadow-VMA can never share a flag
  word with a plain neighbour (mm/vma.c, design sec 3.3).
- interop whitelist, S1-S3 subset (design sec 5): userfaultfd
  register/register_one is rejected on shadow-VMAs -- their faults
  will resolve through the arena layer and never reach
  handle_mm_fault, so uffd could never deliver an event for them
  (fs/userfaultfd.c).
- KUnit: 8 new cases in mm/corten_arena_test.c (suite corten_arena):
  declare_reject, declare_reject_flags, declare_query, release,
  shadow_vma, exit, prctl, concurrent (DECLARE/RELEASE racing QUERY
  and tryget_live on attached kthreads).

Transitional state (review C-1): the S3 delivery state has no fault
consumer yet -- arena-range faults still resolve through the plain
path -- and VMA-splitting operations (mprotect/mremap/MAP_FIXED over a
sub-range) are not rejected until S6.  This is safe today: a split
shadow-VMA keeps VM_CORTEN and there is no arena fault path it could
mislead; the design closes these holes with the S6 rejection hooks
(M3B_DESIGN.md sec 5).

Verification (results/r02/s123-verify.md): =y build clean; diskless
qemu KUnit corten 21 pass + corten_arena 8 pass, two runs, zero
BUG/Oops; corten=on boot smoke green (D9 initcall flip holds, arena
suite 8/8); CORTEN_MM=n link regression clean (exit_mmap hook and the
sys.c case fold away, USERFAULTFD=y control build clean);
checkpatch --strict 0 errors / 0 warnings / 0 checks.

Signed-off-by: purplewall1206 <purplewall1206@github.com>
